  "worked_solution": "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *lower* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (1,4):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {1}^{+}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [a,4]$ and taking the limit as $a\\to {1}^{+}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to {1}^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
